# iyi: a kernel thread in the runtime, and the stop-the-world over them —
# GC_DESIGN.md Stage 4, built on the thread floor's measured mechanism.
#
# `bench/thread_floor.iyi` priced every piece of this before it was
# written: a thread by raw `clone` on Linux costs the floor no name, a
# thread by `pthread_create` on darwin costs libSystem's exact list, a
# `@[ThreadLocal]` is one load, and a stop is one signal per thread with a
# handler that reads the held thread's registers out of the kernel's
# ucontext and parks. This file is that probe moved into the prelude, with
# the two things a runtime needs that a probe does not: a stopped thread's
# registers and stack are roots the collector scans, and a thread inside
# the allocator is not stopped where it stands.
#
#   t = IyiThread.start { work }   # a thread; its body allocates freely
#   t.join                          # Nil; the thread has exited
#
# What a thread is not, said here rather than found: it is not a task. No
# group owns it and nothing cancels it. What crosses threads is what
# `Share` (SPEC.md III.4.4) lets the block capture: `Atomic(T)`, state
# behind a `Mutex(T)` (std/mutex), immutable values, a `Channel(T)` of
# `Share` values (III.4.13), and memory the program keeps straight
# itself. A thread gets a scheduler state when it starts and a heap cache
# on first touch, so it may spawn fibers and it allocates without a lock;
# the world it shares is the centre behind the runtime's one lock, the
# scheduler's lock its wakes cross threads under, and the collector,
# which stops it. The pool parallel tasks run on (III.4.13) is threads of
# this kind, started by the runtime.
#
# **The stop, and what is not stopped.** A collection takes the runtime
# lock, signals every other registered thread, and waits for each to count
# itself in; the handler copies the interrupted registers from the ucontext
# onto the thread's line, records the stack pointer, parks, and counts out
# when the collector releases it. Two threads are not parked where they
# stand. One holding the runtime lock, or between reading a cache list's
# head and popping it — inside `IyiHeap.take`, which sets a word on its
# cache page for the duration — is asked to stop instead: the handler
# leaves a request on the same page and returns, and `take` parks itself
# on the way out, spilling its own callee-saved registers, which is Go's
# "no preemption inside mallocgc" arrived at from the other end (the
# collector holding the lock would otherwise wait on a thread waiting on
# the lock). And one spinning for the lock parks inside the spin, where it
# holds nothing. Everything else — running code, blocked in a syscall the
# signal interrupts (the poller already treats EINTR as a wakeup with no
# news, and SA_RESTART restarts the rest) — stops where it is.
#
# **Where a stopped thread's roots are.** Its registers are the spill on
# its line — the ucontext's general registers, or the callee-saved set a
# voluntary park wrote — and its stack runs from the recorded sp to the
# top of whichever mapping holds it: the thread's own, or a fiber's, found
# by asking every fiber. A thread's main fiber carries the thread's stack
# top, where the main thread's carries 0 for the base `IyiRoots` found.
#
# **A thread's death.** Its body returns, it takes the lock, leaves the
# list a stop walks, and exits; a stop that began before it left waited
# for it, and one that begins after does not name it. On Linux the kernel
# clears the tid word at exit and wakes its futex, which is the join.
#
# Linux x86_64 and aarch64 and darwin arm64, the concurrency targets, and
# only with the collector: a thread under `-Dgc_none` would race the bump
# pointer and one under `-Dgc_boehm` would be a thread Boehm never heard
# of, so neither has one, and `IyiThread` is not a name there.

{% if !flag?(:gc_none) && !flag?(:gc_boehm) && ((flag?(:linux) && (flag?(:x86_64) || flag?(:aarch64))) || (flag?(:darwin) && flag?(:aarch64)) || (flag?(:win32) && flag?(:x86_64))) %}
  # One line per thread, mapped, never heap: a signal handler and a
  # collector both read it, and neither may find it moved or freed.
  IYI_THREAD_LINE       = 512_u64
  IYI_THREAD_STACK      = 1048576_u64
  # +0   the parent's handle: the tid on Linux, written by the kernel at
  #      start and cleared by it at exit; the pthread_t on darwin
  # +8   the next line, from IyiThread.first_line
  # +16  the thread's own stack: its low address, 0 when unknown (darwin)
  # +24  its top: the mapping's end on Linux, `pthread_get_stackaddr_np`'s
  #      on darwin, written by the thread before anything it does allocates
  # +32  the thread's wait for the runtime lock, written by the thread
  #      alone: 1 while it spins for the lock, 3 once a stop parked it
  #      there and until it has tried the lock again (`owe_turn`)
  # +40  where the thread stopped: sp
  # +48  pc
  # +56  the thread's IyiThread object, raw, for the child to find its body
  # +64  set while the thread is exiting: a stop no longer names it
  # +72  this thread's allocator cache, published at registration. Windows
  #      reads it from the other side: a suspended thread cannot answer
  #      "am I inside the allocator?" for itself, and that answer decides
  #      whether the stop may take it where it stands
  # +128 the spill: 32 words for whatever registers the stop captured
  IYI_TL_HANDLE  =   0_u64
  IYI_TL_NEXT    =   8_u64
  IYI_TL_LOW     =  16_u64
  IYI_TL_TOP     =  24_u64
  IYI_TL_LOCKWAIT = 32_u64
  IYI_TL_SP      =  40_u64
  IYI_TL_PC      =  48_u64
  IYI_TL_OBJECT  =  56_u64
  IYI_TL_CACHE   =  72_u64
  IYI_TL_SPILL   = 128_u64
  IYI_TL_SPILL_END = 384_u64

  # The stop's shared words, each on a line of its own on the control
  # page: threads counted in, threads counted out, the release flag, and
  # the count a stopper asleep on one of the first two waits for
  # (`wait_count`), 0 while none sleeps, and the threads a stop parked in
  # the runtime lock's spin that have not tried the lock since
  # (`await_turns`).
  IYI_TC_SUSPENDED = 0_u64
  IYI_TC_RESUMED   = 64_u64
  IYI_TC_RESUME    = 128_u64
  IYI_TC_PID       = 192_u64
  IYI_TC_SLEEPER   = 256_u64
  IYI_TC_TURNS     = 320_u64

  {% if flag?(:linux) %}
    {% if flag?(:x86_64) %}
      IYI_SYS_CLONE        =  56_i64
      IYI_SYS_EXIT         =  60_i64
      IYI_SYS_FUTEX        = 202_i64
      IYI_SYS_GETPID       =  39_i64
      IYI_SYS_GETTID       = 186_i64
      IYI_SYS_TGKILL       = 234_i64
      IYI_SYS_RT_SIGACTION =  13_i64
      IYI_SYS_SCHED_YIELD  =  24_i64
      IYI_SYS_GETAFFINITY  = 204_i64
      # SA_SIGINFO | SA_RESTORER | SA_RESTART: x86_64 has no vdso
      # trampoline the kernel will pick for a handler's return, so the
      # restorer is named; RESTART so a read a stop interrupts resumes
      # rather than answering EINTR to a program that never asked.
      IYI_SA_FLAGS = 0x14000004_u64
      # The kernel's ucontext: uc_flags, uc_link, a 24-byte uc_stack, then
      # sigcontext at 40 — r8..r15, rdi, rsi, rbp, rbx, rdx, rax, rcx, rsp,
      # rip: sixteen registers then the two the scan needs by name.
      IYI_UC_REGS  =  40_u64
      IYI_UC_COUNT =  16_u64
      IYI_UC_SP    = 160_u64
      IYI_UC_PC    = 168_u64
    {% else %}
      IYI_SYS_CLONE        = 220_i64
      IYI_SYS_EXIT         =  93_i64
      IYI_SYS_FUTEX        =  98_i64
      IYI_SYS_GETPID       = 172_i64
      IYI_SYS_GETTID       = 178_i64
      IYI_SYS_TGKILL       = 131_i64
      IYI_SYS_RT_SIGACTION = 134_i64
      IYI_SYS_SCHED_YIELD  = 124_i64
      IYI_SYS_GETAFFINITY  = 123_i64
      # SA_SIGINFO | SA_RESTART; the vdso supplies the return.
      IYI_SA_FLAGS = 0x10000004_u64
      # uc_flags, uc_link, uc_stack, a sigmask padded to 128, sigcontext
      # 16-aligned at 176: fault_address, then x0..x30 at 184, sp, pc.
      IYI_UC_REGS  = 184_u64
      IYI_UC_COUNT =  31_u64
      IYI_UC_SP    = 432_u64
      IYI_UC_PC    = 440_u64
    {% end %}

    # CLONE_VM | CLONE_FS | CLONE_FILES | CLONE_SIGHAND | CLONE_THREAD |
    # CLONE_SYSVSEM | CLONE_SETTLS | CLONE_PARENT_SETTID |
    # CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID | CLONE_CHILD_SETTID. PARENT_SETTID beside the
    # probe's flags: the tid is in the line before `clone` returns to the
    # parent, so a stop that begins the instant after can name the child.
    IYI_CLONE_FLAGS = 0x013d0f00_i64
    IYI_SIGSTOP_THE_WORLD = 10_i64
    IYI_FUTEX_WAIT = 0_i64
    IYI_FUTEX_WAKE = 1_i64

    # `clone` returns twice, and the second return is on a stack the
    # compiler has never heard of, so the child's whole life is inside the
    # asm: it loads the function and argument the parent left at the top
    # of its stack, calls, and exits with the thread-exit syscall. Verbatim
    # from the probe, which found aarch64's argument order the hard way.
    fun __iyi_thread_clone(stack_top : UInt64, entry : UInt64, argument : UInt64, tid_word : UInt64, thread_pointer : UInt64) : Int64
      result = uninitialized Int64
      {% if flag?(:x86_64) %}
        asm("leaq -16(%r12), %rsi
             movq %r13, 0(%rsi)
             movq %r14, 8(%rsi)
             movq $$0x013d0f00, %rdi
             movq %r15, %rdx
             movq %r15, %r10
             movq %rbx, %r8
             movq $$56, %rax
             syscall
             testq %rax, %rax
             jnz 1f
             movq 8(%rsp), %rdi
             movq 0(%rsp), %rax
             andq $$-16, %rsp
             callq *%rax
             movq $$60, %rax
             xorq %rdi, %rdi
             syscall
             1:" : "={rax}"(result)
                  : "{r12}"(stack_top), "{r13}"(entry), "{r14}"(argument), "{r15}"(tid_word), "{rbx}"(thread_pointer)
                  : "rdi", "rsi", "rdx", "r10", "r8", "rcx", "r11", "memory", "cc"
                  : "volatile")
      {% else %}
        asm("sub x1, x19, #16
             stp x20, x21, [x1]
             mov x0, #0x0f00
             movk x0, #0x013d, lsl #16
             mov x2, x22
             mov x3, x23
             mov x4, x22
             mov x8, #220
             svc #0
             cbnz x0, 1f
             ldp x9, x0, [sp], #16
             blr x9
             mov x8, #93
             mov x0, #0
             svc #0
             1:" : "={x0}"(result)
                  : "{x19}"(stack_top), "{x20}"(entry), "{x21}"(argument), "{x22}"(tid_word), "{x23}"(thread_pointer)
                  : "x1", "x2", "x3", "x4", "x8", "x9", "x30", "memory", "cc"
                  : "volatile")
      {% end %}
      result
    end

    # A thread's TLS block, from the executable's own PT_TLS, laid out the
    # way a static libc's startup lays out its own: the segment found by
    # walking the program headers from `__ehdr_start`, the initialised
    # image copied, the rest zero, the thread pointer placed by the ABI's
    # variant — below the block with a self-pointer at `%fs:0` on x86_64,
    # above a 16-byte control block on aarch64.
    class IyiTlsImage
      @@image : UInt64 = 0_u64
      @@filesz : UInt64 = 0_u64
      @@memsz : UInt64 = 0_u64
      @@align : UInt64 = 0_u64

      # The ELF header's own address, the linker's `__ehdr_start`. Read in
      # a def rather than a `fun` so the symbol is referenced only by a
      # program that starts a thread: a `fun` is emitted into every
      # binary, and this one put `__ehdr_start` on every Linux object's
      # undefined list, which the root gate's cross-object audit counts.
      def self.ehdr_start : UInt64
        header = uninitialized UInt64
        {% if flag?(:x86_64) %}
          asm("leaq __ehdr_start(%rip), $0" : "=r"(header) ::: "volatile")
        {% else %}
          asm("adrp $0, __ehdr_start
               add $0, $0, :lo12:__ehdr_start" : "=r"(header) ::: "volatile")
        {% end %}
        header
      end

      def self.read_segment : Nil
        return if @@align != 0_u64
        header = ehdr_start
        phoff = Pointer(UInt64).new(header + 32_u64).value
        phentsize = Pointer(UInt64).new(header + 48_u64).value.unsafe_shr(48_u64)
        phnum = Pointer(UInt64).new(header + 56_u64).value & 0xFFFF_u64
        bias = 0_u64
        found_load = false
        index = 0_u64
        while index < phnum
          entry = header + phoff + index * phentsize
          type = Pointer(UInt64).new(entry).value & 0xFFFFFFFF_u64
          if type == 1_u64 && !found_load
            bias = header - Pointer(UInt64).new(entry + 16_u64).value
            found_load = true
          end
          if type == 7_u64
            @@image = Pointer(UInt64).new(entry + 16_u64).value
            @@filesz = Pointer(UInt64).new(entry + 32_u64).value
            @@memsz = Pointer(UInt64).new(entry + 40_u64).value
            @@align = Pointer(UInt64).new(entry + 48_u64).value
          end
          index = index + 1_u64
        end
        raise "no PT_TLS in the executable" if @@align == 0_u64
        @@image = @@image + bias
      end

      def self.round_up(value : UInt64, align : UInt64) : UInt64
        (value + align - 1_u64) & (0_u64 &- align)
      end

      # A block for one thread; answers the thread pointer to hand `clone`.
      def self.make : UInt64
        read_segment
        size = round_up(@@memsz, @@align)
        mapping = __iyi_mmap(round_up(size + 64_u64, 4096_u64)).address
        {% if flag?(:x86_64) %}
          pointer = round_up(mapping + size, @@align)
          pointer = round_up(pointer, 64_u64)
          block = pointer - size
          Pointer(UInt64).new(pointer).value = pointer
        {% else %}
          pointer = round_up(mapping, 64_u64)
          block = pointer + round_up(16_u64, @@align)
        {% end %}
        index = 0_u64
        while index < @@filesz
          Pointer(UInt8).new(block + index).value = Pointer(UInt8).new(@@image + index).value
          index = index + 1_u64
        end
        pointer
      end
    end

    # The two-instruction sigreturn x86_64's handler returns through.
    {% if flag?(:x86_64) %}
      @[NoInline]
      @[Naked]
      fun __iyi_sigreturn : Nil
        asm("movq $$15, %rax
             syscall" :::: "volatile")
      end

      @[NoInline]
      @[Naked]
      fun __iyi_sigreturn_address : UInt64
        asm("leaq __iyi_sigreturn(%rip), %rax
             retq" :::: "volatile")
        0_u64
      end
    {% end %}
  {% elsif flag?(:darwin) %}
    # darwin: libSystem's thread, by name, the exact list the thread floor
    # measured — `pthread_create`, `pthread_join`, `pthread_kill`,
    # `sigaction` — and the park, which is Linux's on libSystem's public
    # futex (`park`): `os_sync_wait_on_address` and
    # `os_sync_wake_by_address_all`, declared in prelude.iyi beside the
    # mark helpers' park, which needs the same macOS 14.4. Mach's
    # `thread_switch` is a spinner's way of giving its core away
    # (`yield_cpu`).
    lib LibC
      fun pthread_create(thread : UInt64*, attributes : Void*, start : Void*, argument : Void*) : Int32
      fun pthread_join(thread : Void*, value : Void*) : Int32
      fun pthread_kill(thread : Void*, signal : Int32) : Int32
      fun sigaction(signal : Int32, action : Void*, previous : Void*) : Int32
      fun sysctlbyname(name : UInt8*, old : Void*, old_size : UInt64*, new : Void*, new_size : UInt64) : Int32
      fun thread_switch(thread : UInt32, option : Int32, time : UInt32) : Int32
    end

    # <mach/thread_switch.h>: the caller's priority dropped to the lowest
    # for the time given in milliseconds, or until it runs again.
    IYI_SWITCH_OPTION_DEPRESS = 1_i32
    IYI_SIGSTOP_THE_WORLD = 30_i32
    # struct sigaction on darwin arm64 is sixteen bytes: the handler, then
    # `sa_mask` and `sa_flags` packed into the second word. SA_SIGINFO
    # (0x40) | SA_RESTART (0x02), in the high half.
    IYI_SA_WORD = 0x0000004200000000_u64
    # libSystem's ucontext: at 48 a pointer to the mcontext, whose 16-byte
    # exception state precedes x0..x28, fp, lr (31 words at 16), sp at
    # 264, pc at 272.
    IYI_UC_MCONTEXT = 48_u64
    IYI_MC_REGS  =  16_u64
    IYI_MC_COUNT =  31_u64
    IYI_MC_SP    = 264_u64
    IYI_MC_PC    = 272_u64

  {% elsif flag?(:win32) %}
    # The rest of kernel32 is declared in prelude.iyi; these are asked by
    # `core_count` and `first_written` alone.
    lib LibC
      fun GetProcessAffinityMask(process : Void*, process_mask : UInt64*, system_mask : UInt64*) : Int32
      fun VirtualQuery(address : Void*, buffer : Void*, length : UInt64) : UInt64
    end
  {% end %}

  class IyiThread
    # This thread's line; 0 on a thread nobody registered, which is the
    # main thread before the first `start`, and on Linux a child in the
    # instant before its first instruction, found by its tid then. A
    # darwin child is named by no stop until it has written it (`run`).
    @[ThreadLocal]
    @@line : UInt64 = 0_u64
    # Every line, from the newest; the control page; how many lines the
    # list holds, which is how a stop knows whether anybody but itself is
    # there to hear from (`stop_others`); the handler, once.
    @@lines : UInt64 = 0_u64
    @@control : UInt64 = 0_u64
    @@count : Int64 = 0_i64
    @@installed : Bool = false
    {% if flag?(:win32) %}
      # How many threads the last stop left running with a request to
      # park, which is how many it waits for and how many count
      # themselves out of the park after the release.
      @@deferred : Int64 = 0_i64
    {% end %}
    # How many threads the last release let out of their park and the
    # next stop waits to see leave (`resume_others`).
    @@owed : Int64 = 0_i64
    # Every thread object started and not yet running, so a body's closure
    # is rooted from `start` until the thread that runs it holds it on its
    # own stack, and no longer (`run`).
    @@head : IyiThread?

    @line : UInt64
    @proc : Proc(Nil)
    property next_thread : IyiThread?
    # 1 once the body has returned, which a join parks on (`join`).
    @ended : Atomic(UInt64)

    def initialize(@line : UInt64, @proc : Proc(Nil))
      @ended = Atomic(UInt64).new(0_u64)
    end

    def line : UInt64
      @line
    end

    def proc : Proc(Nil)
      @proc
    end

    # ── Entry points, as procs ──────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # The child's first frame and the stop handler are the addresses of
    # two closure-free procs — a proc's first word is its function, called
    # with its arguments in the platform's registers, which is the C
    # convention `clone` and `sigaction` expect. Procs rather than `fun`s
    # because a `fun` is emitted into every program whether or not it is
    # ever called, and `hello` grew 12 KB of thread runtime the day these
    # were `fun`s; a proc named here is codegen'd when `start` is.
    def self.address_of(proc : Proc(UInt64, Nil)) : UInt64
      Pointer(UInt64).new(pointerof(proc).address).value
    end

    def self.address_of_handler(proc : Proc(Int32, Void*, Void*, Nil)) : UInt64
      Pointer(UInt64).new(pointerof(proc).address).value
    end

    def self.thread_entry : UInt64
      {% if flag?(:linux) %}
        address_of(->(line : UInt64) { IyiThread.run(line) })
      {% else %}
        address_of(->(line : UInt64) { IyiThread.run(line) })
      {% end %}
    end

    def self.handler_entry : UInt64
      address_of_handler(->(signal : Int32, info : Void*, context : Void*) { IyiThread.stopped_by_signal(context.address) })
    end

    # ── Lines and the control page ──────────────────────────────────────

    def self.control : UInt64
      @@control = __iyi_mmap(4096_u64).address if @@control == 0
      @@control
    end

    def self.word(address : UInt64) : Pointer(Atomic(UInt64))
      Pointer(Atomic(UInt64)).new(address)
    end

    def self.first_line : UInt64
      @@lines
    end

    def self.line_next(line : UInt64) : UInt64
      IyiHeap.read64(line + IYI_TL_NEXT)
    end

    def self.current_line : UInt64
      @@line
    end

    @@stops : UInt64 = 0_u64
    @@stopped : UInt64 = 0_u64
    @@stop_ns : UInt64 = 0_u64

    # The running thread's stack top, 0 for an unregistered thread, whose
    # stack is the base `IyiRoots` discovered.
    def self.current_stack_top : UInt64
      line = @@line
      return 0_u64 if line == 0
      IyiHeap.read64(line + IYI_TL_TOP)
    end

    # What the stops did, for a program that asks: collections that
    # stopped at least one other thread, and threads stopped in all.
    # Written under the lock, read by whoever.
    def self.stops : UInt64
      @@stops
    end

    def self.stopped : UInt64
      @@stopped
    end

    # Nanoseconds spent from the first signal to the last count-in, in
    # all: the stop half of every pause, on the collector's clock.
    def self.stop_ns : UInt64
      @@stop_ns
    end

    # The program's own threads: every registered one, and the main
    # thread before anything registered it. What the mark beside the
    # program leaves the cores to on Windows.
    def self.program_threads : UInt64
      @@count > 1_i64 ? @@count.to_u64 : 1_u64
    end

    def self.new_line : UInt64
      __iyi_mmap(IYI_THREAD_LINE).address
    end

    # Link a line for the stops, and count it, under the lock: a stop
    # takes the same lock before it walks. The count is the list's and no
    # more, on every platform: a darwin child links and counts its own
    # line (`run`), and `start` counted it before. A thread `start` had
    # counted and that had yet to link was one a stop's early return
    # counted and its walk never found: a collection in that instant got
    # past the return, cleared the counts the last release's threads
    # leave by, signalled nobody, and so released nobody and left the
    # last release's debt standing - and the next stop waited for those
    # threads to count themselves out of a count it had just seen
    # cleared, for good. bench/std_gc_exercise.iyi's main thread
    # allocates as soon as it has started three threads, and on macOS
    # 26.6, an M2 Pro with 10 cores, its plain build hung so in 3 runs
    # of 276, and a copy that wrote the stop's counts out once it had
    # waited 5 s in 3 runs of 302: each time the stop before was the main
    # thread's, finding no line but its own, and the hung one waited for
    # the 3 the release before it had owed (the soak of mt/next at
    # ad0712a65). 7j of bench/thread_exercise.sh makes that instant at
    # will.
    def self.link(line : UInt64) : Nil
      IyiRuntimeLock.lock
      IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_NEXT, @@lines)
      @@lines = line
      @@count = @@count + 1_i64
      IyiRuntimeLock.unlock
    end

    def self.unlink(line : UInt64) : Nil
      IyiRuntimeLock.lock
      previous = 0_u64
      cursor = @@lines
      while cursor != 0
        following = IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_NEXT)
        if cursor == line
          if previous == 0
            @@lines = following
          else
            IyiHeap.write64(previous + IYI_TL_NEXT, following)
          end
          @@count = @@count - 1_i64
          break
        end
        previous = cursor
        cursor = following
      end
      IyiRuntimeLock.unlock
    end

    # The main thread's line, made when the first thread is: its handle
    # and its stack top, which `IyiRoots` found. `@@line` on the thread
    # that calls `start` is then never 0. Its read below, if no thread-local
    # was touched on this thread before, is where darwin makes them all
    # (`run`): before `link` names the line, so no stop's handler does.
    def self.register_current : Nil
      return if @@line != 0
      line = new_line
      {% if flag?(:linux) %}
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_HANDLE, __iyi_conc_syscall3(IYI_SYS_GETTID, 0_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64).to_u64)
      {% elsif flag?(:darwin) %}
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_HANDLE, LibC.pthread_self.address)
      {% elsif flag?(:win32) %}
        real_handle = Pointer(Void).new(0_u64)
        LibC.DuplicateHandle(Pointer(Void).new(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF_u64), LibC.GetCurrentThread,
          Pointer(Void).new(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF_u64), pointerof(real_handle), 0_i32, 0_i32, 2_i32)
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_HANDLE, real_handle.address)
      {% end %}
      # Windows reads this thread's allocator state from another thread,
      # so the cache has to exist and be findable before the first stop
      # can name this line. Made here rather than on first allocation:
      # one page, once per thread.
      {% if flag?(:win32) %}
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_CACHE, IyiHeap.cache_page)
      {% end %}
      IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_TOP, IyiRoots.stack_base)
      @@line = line
      link(line)
    end

    # ── Start, run, join ────────────────────────────────────────────────

    def self.start(&block : -> Nil) : IyiThread
      start(block)
    end

    def self.start(proc : Proc(Nil)) : IyiThread
      register_current
      install_handler
      # A second scheduler thread is coming: from here fibers on two
      # threads may wake each other, and the scheduler's lock is taken
      # (SPEC.md III.4.13). Said before the thread exists, so it never
      # runs a fiber the lock does not cover.
      IyiScheduler.expect_thread
      line = new_line
      thread = IyiThread.new(line, proc)
      IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_OBJECT, thread.object_id)
      # Rooted before anything else can run: the list is a class variable.
      IyiRuntimeLock.lock
      thread.next_thread = @@head
      @@head = thread
      IyiRuntimeLock.unlock
      {% if flag?(:linux) %}
        stack = __iyi_mmap(IYI_THREAD_STACK).address
        __iyi_conc_syscall3(IYI_SYS_MPROTECT, stack.to_i64, 4096_i64, 0_i64)
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_LOW, stack)
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_TOP, stack + IYI_THREAD_STACK)
        # Linked before the clone, under the lock the clone is made under:
        # a stop cannot begin between the two, and the tid is in the line
        # before the lock is released.
        IyiRuntimeLock.lock
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_NEXT, @@lines)
        @@lines = line
        @@count = @@count + 1_i64
        result = __iyi_thread_clone(stack + IYI_THREAD_STACK, thread_entry, line, line + IYI_TL_HANDLE, IyiTlsImage.make)
        IyiRuntimeLock.unlock
        raise "clone failed: #{result}" if result < 0
      {% elsif flag?(:darwin) %}
        # Linked and counted by the thread itself, once `@@line` is
        # written (`run`), so no stop signals it before. Created under the
        # lock all the same: `pthread_create` writes the handle into the
        # line after the thread exists, and the thread's own link waits on
        # this lock, so the handle is there before a stop can read it.
        IyiRuntimeLock.lock
        result = LibC.pthread_create(Pointer(UInt64).new(line + IYI_TL_HANDLE), Pointer(Void).new(0_u64),
          Pointer(Void).new(thread_entry), Pointer(Void).new(line))
        IyiRuntimeLock.unlock
        raise "pthread_create failed: #{result}" if result != 0
      {% elsif flag?(:win32) %}
        # Created suspended (CREATE_SUSPENDED, 4) and its handle written
        # under the lock, so no stop can name the line before it can
        # suspend the thread. The handle was written after the unlock while
        # the thread already ran: a stop between the two suspended NULL,
        # read sp 0, and scanned from address 0, and with 2 ms of busy-wait
        # there five runs in five died of a memory fault inside a second.
        IyiRuntimeLock.lock
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_NEXT, @@lines)
        @@lines = line
        @@count = @@count + 1_i64
        h = LibC.CreateThread(nil, 0_u64, Pointer(Void).new(thread_entry), Pointer(Void).new(line), 4_i32, nil)
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_HANDLE, h.address)
        IyiRuntimeLock.unlock
        raise "CreateThread failed" if h.address == 0_u64
        LibC.ResumeThread(h)
      {% end %}
      thread
    end

    # A helper: a kernel thread the collector owns and the stop never
    # names — a mark worker. No line, no `IyiThread` object, no cache: it
    # allocates nothing from the heap, runs `entry` with `argument` for
    # its whole life, and is never joined. Its thread-locals are its own
    # (a TLS block on Linux, dyld's on darwin), which is what lets the
    # marker keep per-worker state in `@[ThreadLocal]` variables.
    def self.start_helper(entry : UInt64, argument : UInt64) : Nil
      {% if flag?(:linux) %}
        stack = __iyi_mmap(IYI_THREAD_STACK).address
        __iyi_madvise_nohuge(stack, IYI_THREAD_STACK)
        __iyi_conc_syscall3(IYI_SYS_MPROTECT, stack.to_i64, 4096_i64, 0_i64)
        # The tid word is the stack's guard-side scratch: nobody joins.
        scratch = __iyi_mmap(4096_u64).address
        result = __iyi_thread_clone(stack + IYI_THREAD_STACK, entry, argument, scratch, IyiTlsImage.make)
        raise "clone failed: #{result}" if result < 0
      {% elsif flag?(:darwin) %}
        handle = uninitialized UInt64
        result = LibC.pthread_create(pointerof(handle), Pointer(Void).new(0_u64), Pointer(Void).new(entry), Pointer(Void).new(argument))
        raise "pthread_create failed: #{result}" if result != 0
      {% elsif flag?(:win32) %}
        h = LibC.CreateThread(nil, 0_u64, Pointer(Void).new(entry), Pointer(Void).new(argument), 0_i32, nil)
        raise "CreateThread failed" if h.address == 0_u64
        # Without the boost Windows gives a thread whose wait is satisfied.
        # The program's own thread wakes the helpers for a mark beside it
        # (`IyiMark.wake_helpers`), and with the boost each woken helper
        # ran above it and took its core: measured on a twelve-core
        # machine with eleven helpers, the wake held the program's thread
        # 3.7 to 40 ms, while the thread did no work in that time, where
        # without it the wake takes 12 to 20 us. Binary trees' median went
        # from 0.171 s to 0.147 and its total pause from 8.8 ms to 3.5.
        LibC.SetThreadPriorityBoost(h, 1_i32)
      {% end %}
    end

    # The child's whole life: name its line, find its body on the rooted
    # list, run it, leave the stop's list, and return into the exit the
    # clone asm (or pthread) performs.
    def self.run(line : UInt64) : Nil
      # darwin: the stack's top before anything here allocates. It was
      # written below `IyiStackGuard.install`, whose `stack_t` is a heap
      # chunk, and a stop between that allocation and the three stores
      # scanned this stack from sp up to 0 - nothing. `GC.collect` sweeps
      # inside its pause, so the unmarked chunk went onto a free batch
      # there, and the stores after it wrote the batch's chain: the
      # centre's next pop of the class read address 0x10008, the
      # `stack_t`'s 65,536 plus 8, and the process died of SIGSEGV.
      # Threads born sixteen at a time beside a thread collecting in a
      # loop (bench/thread_exercise.sh, 7d) died so on macos-15's three
      # cores in 6 of 115 runs of 3,200 threads before the fix below
      # (`@@line`'s) and 5 of 103 after it; with the top first, in none of
      # 128. The top is `pthread_get_stackaddr_np`'s, as the main thread's
      # is, rather than a local's address in this frame: the scan covers
      # `run`'s own frame whole.
      {% if flag?(:darwin) %}
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_TOP, IyiRoots.discover_stack_base)
      {% end %}
      @@line = line
      # darwin makes a thread's thread-locals at its first touch of one:
      # the store above, where dyld `calloc`s one block for every
      # `@[ThreadLocal]` in the image (three `__thread` variables in C sat
      # in one 128-byte block on macOS 26.6), the two the stop's handler
      # reads among them - `@@line` and the heap's `@@cache`. The parent
      # linked this line before `pthread_create`, so a stop could signal
      # the thread inside that `calloc`, under libmalloc's lock; the
      # handler's read made the block again, took the lock the
      # interrupted call held, and libplatform killed the process
      # ("Trying to recursively lock an os_unfair_lock"). So the thread
      # links and counts its own line now (`link`), with its thread-locals
      # made: no stop names it before, and nothing it did before allocates
      # from the heap or holds a pointer into it.
      {% if flag?(:darwin) %}
        link(line)
      {% end %}
      # An alternate signal stack is a thread's own, so a stack overflow on
      # this thread has somewhere to say so (`IyiStackGuard`).
      {% unless flag?(:win32) %}
        IyiStackGuard.install
      {% end %}
      # Windows' equivalent, and just as much a thread's own:
      # `SetThreadStackGuarantee` keeps room under the guard page for the
      # handler that names the overflow, and the 16 KB were set on the main
      # thread alone. Measured, a thread without it came into
      # `__iyi_win_fault` with 6,736 to 9,472 bytes committed under it, the
      # dispatcher having taken 2,672 of what the overflow left; with it,
      # 14,864 to 17,760. On the Windows runners the thread's overflow was
      # now and then reported as a memory fault, and the main thread's and
      # a fiber's, each with 16 KB, never were (`bench/panics.sh`). Set
      # before anything else runs here, on the thread's own stack, before
      # any fiber rewrites the bounds the TEB holds.
      {% if flag?(:win32) %}
        guarantee = 16384_i32
        LibC.SetThreadStackGuarantee(pointerof(guarantee))
      {% end %}
      {% if flag?(:win32) %}
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_TOP, IyiRoots.discover_stack_base)
        # Before the body runs, so the first stop that names this line
        # reads a cache and not a zero (see `suspend_others`).
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_CACHE, IyiHeap.cache_page)
      {% end %}
      # Its scheduler state, made before the body runs: a deadlock's walk
      # of the states counts this thread as coming until it has one, and
      # as busy from then until it waits in its own poller (III.4.13).
      IyiScheduler.adopt_thread
      # Found and taken off the list under the lock, which every writer of
      # the list holds; from here this frame roots the body. Left on it,
      # every thread ever started kept its block's captures: 2,000 threads
      # each capturing 64 KiB, every one joined, kept 156 MiB.
      IyiRuntimeLock.lock
      previous = nil.as(IyiThread?)
      thread = @@head
      while thread.is_a?(IyiThread)
        break if thread.line == line
        previous = thread
        thread = thread.next_thread
      end
      if thread.is_a?(IyiThread)
        if previous.is_a?(IyiThread)
          previous.next_thread = thread.next_thread
        else
          @@head = thread.next_thread
        end
        thread.next_thread = nil
      end
      IyiRuntimeLock.unlock
      raise "a thread started with no body" unless thread.is_a?(IyiThread)
      thread.proc.call
      # The body is over: a join waiting on it is woken here, parked
      # rather than held in the kernel (`join`), and what the join still
      # waits for in the kernel is the retire below and the exit.
      thread.finish
      # The fibers go first, while the stack they name is still this
      # thread's (`IyiScheduler.retire_thread`).
      IyiScheduler.retire_thread
      # The cache goes before it is unmapped: a stop that read this line
      # after the retire would follow a pointer into a freed mapping.
      {% if flag?(:win32) %}
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_CACHE, 0_u64)
      {% end %}
      IyiHeap.retire_cache
      unlink(line)
    end

    # :nodoc: — `run`'s, once the body has returned: every join parked
    # on it woken.
    def finish : Nil
      @ended.set(1_u64)
      IyiScheduler.wake_on(pointerof(@ended), 0x7FFFFFFF)
    end

    # Waits for the thread to end, then unmaps its line; a thread joined
    # already answers at once. A second join on Windows waited on, and
    # closed, whatever handle had the first one's value by then: with a
    # thread started after the first join and sleeping 2 s, it took 2,000
    # ms, and that thread's own join then returned on the closed handle.
    #
    # The body's run is waited for parked (`IyiScheduler.park_on`), so the
    # thread joining runs its other tasks meanwhile - a pool worker joining
    # one held every task in its ring in the kernel with it - and the
    # kernel's join then waits only for the end `finish` is followed by.
    # A cancel does not end the wait, which has no `Cancelled` to answer.
    def join : Nil
      line = @line
      return if line == 0
      while @ended.get == 0_u64
        IyiScheduler.park_on(pointerof(@ended), 0_u64, false)
      end
      {% if flag?(:linux) %}
        while (tid = IyiThread.word(line + IYI_TL_HANDLE).value.get) != 0_u64
          __iyi_conc_syscall6(IYI_SYS_FUTEX, (line + IYI_TL_HANDLE).to_i64, IYI_FUTEX_WAIT, tid.to_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64)
        end
      {% elsif flag?(:darwin) %}
        handle = Pointer(Void).new(IyiHeap.read64(line + IYI_TL_HANDLE))
        result = LibC.pthread_join(handle, Pointer(Void).new(0_u64))
        raise "pthread_join failed: #{result}" if result != 0
      {% elsif flag?(:win32) %}
        handle = Pointer(Void).new(IyiHeap.read64(line + IYI_TL_HANDLE))
        LibC.WaitForSingleObject(handle, -1_i32)
        LibC.CloseHandle(handle)
      {% end %}
      # The thread has left the stop's list and ended, so nothing reads
      # the line again: each thread's stayed mapped for good.
      @line = 0_u64
      __iyi_munmap(line, IYI_THREAD_LINE)
    end

    # The deferred stop, paid where a thread holds nothing: `take`'s exit,
    # and the runtime lock's spin. The request is cleared before the park
    # so the exit after a spin's park does not park twice.
    # `@[NoInline]`, and load-bearing: the spill below captures the
    # callee-saved registers, and the call boundary is what puts every
    # live value the caller held in a caller-saved register onto its
    # stack first. Inlined into `take`'s exit, the chunk `take` was about
    # to return sat in a scratch register nothing scanned, and the mark
    # that stopped the thread there freed it.
    @[NoInline]
    def self.park_if_requested : Nil
      return unless IyiHeap.stop_requested?
      IyiHeap.clear_request
      stop_here
    end

    # ── The stop ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    def self.install_handler : Nil
      return if @@installed
      @@installed = true
      {% if flag?(:linux) %}
        IyiHeap.write64(control + IYI_TC_PID, __iyi_conc_syscall3(IYI_SYS_GETPID, 0_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64).to_u64)
        action = Pointer(UInt64).malloc(4_u64)
        action[0] = handler_entry
        action[1] = IYI_SA_FLAGS
        {% if flag?(:x86_64) %}
          action[2] = __iyi_sigreturn_address
        {% else %}
          action[2] = 0_u64
        {% end %}
        action[3] = 0_u64
        result = __iyi_conc_syscall6(IYI_SYS_RT_SIGACTION, IYI_SIGSTOP_THE_WORLD, action.address.to_i64, 0_i64, 8_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64)
        raise "rt_sigaction failed: #{result}" if result < 0
      {% elsif flag?(:darwin) %}
        action = Pointer(UInt64).malloc(2_u64)
        action[0] = handler_entry
        action[1] = IYI_SA_WORD
        result = LibC.sigaction(IYI_SIGSTOP_THE_WORLD, action.as(Void*), Pointer(Void).new(0_u64))
        raise "sigaction failed: #{result}" if result != 0
      {% elsif flag?(:win32) %}
        # Windows uses SuspendThread / ResumeThread directly
      {% end %}
    end

    {% if flag?(:win32) %}
      # The stopping thread runs at Windows' highest priority from before
      # it takes the runtime lock for a collection until it lets that
      # lock go after its release, the pause between included, and is
      # put back then (`raise_stopper`, `lower_stopper`, `IyiMark.collect`,
      # `IyiMark.unlock_released`). A stop that is not a collection's
      # thread's - a helper's - raises at the stop.
      # A stop here is that thread's own loop of `SuspendThread` and
      # `ResumeThread`, and a thread above it on its processor held the
      # world stopped until it let go: in iyiturkiye on a saturated machine
      # (Windows 11, 12 logical processors, the user's machine) single
      # calls in the loop took 148 to 214 ms, and of the stops past 2 ms
      # in three runs, 17 of 145 passed 10 ms on master, 13 of them inside
      # the suspends. Raised, 4 of 127 passed it, the longest 33 ms and
      # waiting in `GetThreadContext`, and the longest single call took
      # 26 us. bench/thread_exercise.sh's burst on the stopping thread's
      # processor holds a stop for 100 ms without the raise and under 2 ms
      # with it.
      #
      # Windows only: a POSIX stop is a signal per thread, and an
      # unprivileged Linux thread cannot raise its own priority; neither
      # was measured. A thread already at the highest or above is left so.
      THREAD_PRIORITY_HIGHEST = 2_i32
      @@stopper_priority : Int32 = 0_i32
      @@stopper_id : Int32 = 0_i32
      @@stopper_raised = false

      def self.raise_stopper : Nil
        me = LibC.GetCurrentThread
        priority = LibC.GetThreadPriority(me)
        return if priority >= THREAD_PRIORITY_HIGHEST
        @@stopper_priority = priority
        @@stopper_id = LibC.GetCurrentThreadId
        @@stopper_raised = LibC.SetThreadPriority(me, THREAD_PRIORITY_HIGHEST) != 0
      end

      # The raise put back, by the thread that made it and by no other:
      # after the collection lets the runtime lock go
      # (`IyiMark.unlock_released` says why not inside the release), and
      # in `raise` for a panic inside a stop, which ends only its task and
      # leaves the thread to run the tasks after it.
      def self.lower_stopper : Nil
        return unless @@stopper_raised && @@stopper_id == LibC.GetCurrentThreadId
        @@stopper_raised = false
        LibC.SetThreadPriority(LibC.GetCurrentThread, @@stopper_priority)
      end

      # Windows has no signal to deliver: the thread that stops the others
      # suspends them where they stand. The one check the POSIX handler
      # makes on the thread itself — never park a thread inside the
      # allocator, because a `take` half done holds a chunk in a register
      # the scan has no name for and may hold the centre's lock against
      # the sweep — is made from here instead, off the cache the stopped
      # thread published on its line.
      #
      # A thread found inside gets the request and its suspend back, and
      # parks itself at `leave`, which is exactly POSIX's deferral. The
      # first shape of this fix took the suspend back and simply retried,
      # and four threads allocating never left the retry: somebody was
      # always inside. The request converges because `leave` runs on every
      # exit from the allocator and the lock's spin parks too.
      private def self.resume_suspended(line : UInt64) : Nil
        cursor = @@lines
        while cursor != 0
          if cursor != line
            LibC.ResumeThread(Pointer(Void).new(IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_HANDLE)))
          end
          cursor = IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_NEXT)
        end
      end

      # Answers how many threads were left running with a request to park,
      # which is how many the stop then waits to hear from. The allocator's
      # word is read after `GetThreadContext`, which waits for the suspend
      # `SuspendThread` only asks for. Read between the two, it let a thread
      # run on into `take` and be stopped there: thread_exercise's switching
      # program died of a memory fault once in the 54 runs CI made of it, and
      # 15 times in 20 here with 20 us of that run-on made certain.
      #
      # Every suspend is asked for before any is waited for. A thread that
      # is not running takes its suspend when the scheduler next runs it,
      # and `GetThreadContext` waits for that: one suspend and one wait at a
      # time made a stop the sum of those waits. Beside 48 threads computing
      # on twelve cores a stop took 85 to 275 ms that way, and 0 to 13 asked
      # all at once (bench/thread_exercise.sh).
      private def self.suspend_others(line : UInt64) : Int64
        cursor = @@lines
        while cursor != 0
          LibC.SuspendThread(Pointer(Void).new(IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_HANDLE))) if cursor != line
          cursor = IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_NEXT)
        end
        deferred = 0_i64
        cursor = @@lines
        while cursor != 0
          if cursor != line
            handle = Pointer(Void).new(IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_HANDLE))
            sp = IyiRoots.capture_thread_registers(handle, cursor + IYI_TL_SPILL)
            cache = IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_CACHE)
            if IyiHeap.cache_inside?(cache)
              IyiHeap.request_stop_at(cache)
              LibC.ResumeThread(handle)
              deferred = deferred + 1_i64
            else
              IyiHeap.write64(cursor + IYI_TL_SP, sp)
            end
          end
          cursor = IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_NEXT)
        end
        deferred
      end
    {% end %}

    # The thread that runs this holds the runtime lock. Every other
    # registered thread is signalled and waited for; when this returns,
    # each has its registers and sp on its line and is parked. Answers
    # how many stopped, which is how many `resume_others` releases.
    def self.stop_others : Int64
      # A helper has no line and stops every registered thread; a thread
      # with a line stops every other.
      line = @@line
      return 0_i64 if @@count == 0_i64 || (line != 0 && @@count <= 1_i64)
      {% if flag?(:win32) %}
        # Raised before the stop's clock starts, so all of what that clock
        # counts runs above the program's other threads.
        raise_stopper
      {% end %}
      started = IyiMark.now_ns
      page = control
      # The last stop's threads out of their park before its words are
      # cleared: one still inside reads the release word, and cleared
      # under it, the release it was given is taken back. On Windows the
      # raise above covers this wait too.
      {% if flag?(:win32) %}
        while word(page + IYI_TC_RESUMED).value.get < @@owed.to_u64
          yield_cpu
        end
      {% else %}
        wait_count(page + IYI_TC_RESUMED, @@owed.to_u64)
      {% end %}
      word(page + IYI_TC_SUSPENDED).value.set(0_u64)
      word(page + IYI_TC_RESUMED).value.set(0_u64)
      word(page + IYI_TC_RESUME).value.set(0_u64)
      {% if flag?(:win32) %}
        # Every other thread is either stopped where it stood, with its
        # registers on its line, or running with a request to park. Only
        # the second kind is waited for, and only it counts itself in.
        others = @@count - (line != 0 ? 1_i64 : 0_i64)
        deferred = suspend_others(line)
        @@deferred = deferred
        while word(page + IYI_TC_SUSPENDED).value.get < deferred.to_u64
          yield_cpu
        end
      {% else %}
        others = 0_i64
        cursor = @@lines
        while cursor != 0
          if cursor != line
            {% if flag?(:linux) %}
              tid = IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_HANDLE).to_i64
              result = __iyi_conc_syscall3(IYI_SYS_TGKILL, IyiHeap.read64(page + IYI_TC_PID).to_i64, tid, IYI_SIGSTOP_THE_WORLD)
              raise "tgkill failed: #{result}" if result < 0
            {% elsif flag?(:darwin) %}
              handle = Pointer(Void).new(IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_HANDLE))
              result = LibC.pthread_kill(handle, IYI_SIGSTOP_THE_WORLD)
              raise "pthread_kill failed: #{result}" if result != 0
            {% end %}
            others = others + 1_i64
          end
          cursor = IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_NEXT)
        end
        wait_count(page + IYI_TC_SUSPENDED, others.to_u64)
      {% end %}
      @@stops = @@stops + 1_u64
      @@stopped = @@stopped + others.to_u64
      @@stop_ns = @@stop_ns + (IyiMark.now_ns - started).to_u64
      others
    end

    # The release, and no wait for the released to leave their park: the
    # next stop waits for that before it clears the words they read
    # (`stop_others`), and until then each runs as soon as it has a core.
    # Waited for here, the collection lasted until the last of them had a
    # processor again. On a ten-core M2 Pro (macOS 26.6), pool workers
    # stopped while they ran came back late at every stop, and the
    # release waited 3.5 ms a collection for them (eight workers beside
    # two allocating tasks, 23 collections, 81 ms of 92 ms of pauses);
    # and on Windows 11, 12 logical processors, a thread parked inside
    # the allocator and then held off its processor by another program
    # kept each collection 403 to 410 ms past its stop, 25 to 28 now
    # (bench/thread_exercise.sh, step 4e, the mark of 2,000,000 links
    # among it).
    def self.resume_others(others : Int64) : Nil
      return if others == 0_i64
      page = control
      word(page + IYI_TC_RESUME).value.set(1_u64)
      {% if flag?(:win32) %}
        # A thread that parked itself is not suspended, and `ResumeThread`
        # on a suspend count of zero is nothing: the flag above releases
        # the one and this releases the other. Only the parked ones count
        # themselves out.
        resume_suspended(@@line)
        @@owed = @@deferred
      {% else %}
        {% if flag?(:linux) %}
          __iyi_conc_syscall6(IYI_SYS_FUTEX, (page + IYI_TC_RESUME).to_i64, IYI_FUTEX_WAKE, 1000000_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64)
        {% else %}
          # The wake answers -1 with errno ENOENT when no thread has
          # reached its wait yet, and this runs inside an allocation, which
          # may fall between a failed call of the program's and its look
          # at errno.
          errno = LibC.error_location.value
          LibC.os_sync_wake_by_address_all(Pointer(Void).new(page + IYI_TC_RESUME), 8_u64, 0_u32)
          LibC.error_location.value = errno
        {% end %}
        @@owed = others
      {% end %}
    end

    # The stopper's wait for its threads to count in, and at the next
    # stop's start for the last release's to have counted out: a spin of
    # `STOP_SPINS` pause hints, then a sleep on the count's own word,
    # which the thread that brings it to `target` wakes (`count_in`). The
    # stopper says what it waits for on `IYI_TC_SLEEPER` before it looks
    # at the count a last time, and a thread counts itself before it
    # looks there, so either the stopper sees the last count and does not
    # sleep, or the last thread sees the stopper and wakes it; the kernel
    # sleeps it only while the word still holds the count it saw.
    #
    # This was a loop around `yield_cpu`, which is the syscall on Linux
    # and the ARM hint on darwin. The hint gives nothing away, so a stop
    # spun through the timeslice of the very thread it waited for; the
    # syscall gives the core to whoever the scheduler has waiting, and
    # beside busy processes that was one of them for a slice, every turn.
    # Asleep, the stopper's core is the stopped threads' to count in on.
    # Measured with thread_exercise 7d's storm (threads born sixteen at a
    # time beside a thread collecting in a loop, 200 rounds or 10 s), on
    # Linux (12 vCPU, shared with other jobs), five runs each, interleaved
    # (MtDspin's final2): beside nine busy processes it made 848 to 1,184
    # threads in its 10 s with the yield loop and all 3,200 in 5.8 to 8.6 s
    # with the sleep; beside twelve, 480 to 880 against 2,400 to 2,784;
    # bench/thread_exercise.iyi at 8 and 20 threads held level.
    #
    # 1,024 turns before the sleep: about 20 to 30 us on either machine
    # is what a stop of a busy thread takes (`stop_ns`), and a stop that
    # is over inside the spin costs nobody a syscall.
    STOP_SPINS = 1024_i64

    def self.wait_count(address : UInt64, target : UInt64) : Nil
      turns = 0_i64
      while (now = word(address).value.get) < target
        turns = turns &+ 1
        if turns < STOP_SPINS
          spin_pause
          next
        end
        word(control + IYI_TC_SLEEPER).value.set(target)
        next if word(address).value.get != now
        {% if flag?(:linux) %}
          __iyi_conc_syscall6(IYI_SYS_FUTEX, address.to_i64, IYI_FUTEX_WAIT, now.to_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64)
        {% else %}
          # A wait answers -1 with errno: EINTR, or EFAULT for a page not
          # resident; this runs inside an allocation, so errno is put back.
          errno = LibC.error_location.value
          LibC.os_sync_wait_on_address(Pointer(Void).new(address), now, 8_u64, 0_u32)
          LibC.error_location.value = errno
        {% end %}
      end
      word(control + IYI_TC_SLEEPER).value.set(0_u64) if turns >= STOP_SPINS
    end

    # A stopped thread counting itself in or out (`park`), and waking the
    # stopper if it sleeps on this count and this thread is its last.
    def self.count_in(address : UInt64) : Nil
      now = word(address).value.add(1_u64) &+ 1_u64
      {% if flag?(:linux) || flag?(:darwin) %}
        sleeper = word(control + IYI_TC_SLEEPER).value.get
        return if sleeper == 0_u64 || now < sleeper
        {% if flag?(:linux) %}
          __iyi_conc_syscall6(IYI_SYS_FUTEX, address.to_i64, IYI_FUTEX_WAKE, 1_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64)
        {% else %}
          # The wake answers -1 with errno ENOENT when the stopper has not
          # reached its wait yet, and this runs in the stop's handler.
          errno = LibC.error_location.value
          LibC.os_sync_wake_by_address_all(Pointer(Void).new(address), 8_u64, 0_u32)
          LibC.error_location.value = errno
        {% end %}
      {% end %}
    end

    # ── The turn a released lock waiter is owed ─────────────────────────
    #
    # The release no longer waits for anybody, and a thread collecting in
    # a loop let the runtime lock go and took it back before the threads
    # it had released were awake: the ones waiting for that lock to start
    # or end a thread were stopped again before they had tried it once.
    # On a ten-core M2 Pro (macOS 26.6), threads started sixteen at a
    # time beside such a thread hung past 60 s (bench/thread_exercise.sh,
    # step 7d). A wait before the lock for every released thread to leave
    # its park was not enough - a thread out of its park has not yet
    # reached the lock, and the collector, spinning on the count, got
    # there first - and the same program made 320 threads in 10 to 17 s
    # against 3,200 in about 3 s with the wait in the release.
    #
    # So a thread a stop parks in the lock's spin owes a try of the lock,
    # counted on the control page before it counts itself in, and pays
    # when it next takes the lock or finds it held. The collection waits
    # for the debts off the lock, before it takes it (`await_turns`): the
    # threads it released are then at the lock, competing for it, as they
    # were when the release waited. Only those threads owe: one blocked in
    # the kernel or computing is not starved by a stop, and waiting for
    # its turn would hold every collection for nothing.

    # A park inside the lock's spin. The word is the thread's own, and
    # written in its handler: `pay_turn` swaps it, so a stop landing in
    # the middle of a payment neither loses a debt nor counts one twice.
    def self.owe_turn(line : UInt64, page : UInt64) : Nil
      return if line == 0 || IyiHeap.read64(line + IYI_TL_LOCKWAIT) != 1_u64
      IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_LOCKWAIT, 3_u64)
      word(page + IYI_TC_TURNS).value.add(1_u64)
    end

    # A try of the lock after the park: the thread still spinning
    # (`waiting` 1) or holding it (0).
    def self.pay_turn(line : UInt64, waiting : UInt64) : Nil
      if word(line + IYI_TL_LOCKWAIT).value.swap(waiting) == 3_u64
        word(control + IYI_TC_TURNS).value.sub(1_u64)
      end
    end

    # The lock taken after a spin (`IyiRuntimeLock.lock`).
    def self.lock_taken : Nil
      line = @@line
      pay_turn(line, 0_u64) if line != 0
    end

    # How long a collection waits for the debts before it takes the lock
    # anyway: a thread owing a turn and held off every core pays nothing
    # until it has one, and the collection is not its to wait out.
    TURN_WAIT_NS = 2_000_000_u64

    # Before a collection takes the runtime lock to stop the program.
    def self.await_turns : Nil
      page = @@control
      return if page == 0
      turns = word(page + IYI_TC_TURNS)
      return if turns.value.get == 0_u64
      ends = IyiMark.now_ns + TURN_WAIT_NS
      while turns.value.get != 0_u64 && IyiMark.now_ns < ends
        yield_cpu
      end
    end

    # The cores this process may run on: what the marker sizes its
    # helpers by. Linux asks `sched_getaffinity` for the mask and counts
    # its bits; darwin asks `sysctlbyname("hw.ncpu")` — no new name there,
    # libSystem's, listed in the floor's darwin set with this as its
    # reason. The mask lives in a mapping, not the heap: the caller is the
    # collector, holding the runtime lock inside an allocation, and an
    # allocation here would refill against that lock and never return.
    def self.core_count : UInt64
      {% if flag?(:win32) %}
        # The process's affinity mask, counted, as Linux counts its own:
        # the machine's count started eleven helpers for a process held to
        # one core (`start /affinity 1`), and eight allocating threads ran
        # 3,077 ms against 178 with none. A process across processor
        # groups has no single mask (both read 0), and gets the machine's.
        mask = 0_u64
        system = 0_u64
        count = 0_u64
        if LibC.GetProcessAffinityMask(LibC.GetCurrentProcess, pointerof(mask), pointerof(system)) != 0
          while mask != 0_u64
            count = count + (mask & 1_u64)
            mask = mask.unsafe_shr(1_u64)
          end
        end
        count = LibC.GetActiveProcessorCount(0xFFFF_u16).to_u64 if count == 0_u64
        count == 0_u64 ? 1_u64 : count
      {% elsif flag?(:linux) %}
        mask = Pointer(UInt64).new(__iyi_mmap(4096_u64).address)
        got = __iyi_conc_syscall3(IYI_SYS_GETAFFINITY, 0_i64, 128_i64, mask.address.to_i64)
        return 1_u64 if got <= 0
        words = got.to_u64.unsafe_div(8_u64)
        count = 0_u64
        index = 0_u64
        while index < words
          word = (mask + index.to_i64).value
          while word != 0_u64
            count = count + (word & 1_u64)
            word = word.unsafe_shr(1_u64)
          end
          index = index + 1_u64
        end
        count == 0_u64 ? 1_u64 : count
      {% else %}
        value = 0_i64
        size = 8_u64
        result = LibC.sysctlbyname("hw.ncpu".to_unsafe, pointerof(value).as(Void*), pointerof(size), Pointer(Void).new(0_u64), 0_u64)
        return 1_u64 if result != 0 || value <= 0
        value.to_u64
      {% end %}
    end

    # A spin's breath: the CPU's pause hint, no syscall.
    def self.spin_pause : Nil
      {% if flag?(:x86_64) %}
        asm("pause" :::: "volatile")
      {% else %}
        asm("yield" :::: "volatile")
      {% end %}
    end

    def self.yield_cpu : Nil
      {% if flag?(:win32) %}
        LibC.SwitchToThread
      {% elsif flag?(:linux) %}
        __iyi_conc_syscall3(IYI_SYS_SCHED_YIELD, 0_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64)
      {% else %}
        # Mach's `thread_switch` with no thread named and the depress
        # option: the core to whichever thread the scheduler has waiting,
        # this one's priority at the lowest for up to a millisecond. This
        # was the ARM `yield` hint, which gives the core's other hardware
        # thread a turn and nothing to the scheduler, so every loop around
        # it - the runtime lock's spinner, a class lock's, the scheduler's,
        # the stop's wait for its threads - spun hot through the timeslice
        # of the very thread it waited for. A thread with nothing to do
        # until somebody tells it still parks - a stopped thread on the
        # stop's release word (`park`), a mark helper on the generation
        # word (`IyiMark.wait_generation`).
        #
        # POSIX's `sched_yield` was the first try (DarwinSoak's branch
        # exp/darwin-sched-yield, and MtDspin's runs on macOS 26.6, an M2
        # Pro with 10 cores): it is `swtch_pri(0)` in libSystem, whose
        # depression lasts until the thread runs again, and beside a busy
        # process per core, where something always runs, that was a
        # timeslice each: bench/thread_exercise.iyi at 20 threads took 6.0
        # to 10.2 s against the hint's 4.3 to 5.8 (run6, five runs each).
        # The millisecond's bound took that to 5.9 to 7.6, and the turns
        # before the first give-away (`YIELD_AFTER`) took the rest.
        LibC.thread_switch(0_u32, IYI_SWITCH_OPTION_DEPRESS, 1_u32)
      {% end %}
    end

    # The turns a spinner takes on the pause hint before it first gives
    # its core away (`yield_cpu`), and then every 128th. On x86 a `pause`
    # is some 35 ns, so 128 turns are a few microseconds; darwin's
    # `yield` hint is next to nothing on the M2, and 128 of them gave the
    # core away before a holder running on another core had time to let
    # go. With 1,024: at 20 threads beside a busy process per core 1.7 to
    # 3.5 s against 4.8 to 8.9 (run7), and alone 1.5 to 3.7 against 1.0
    # to 1.5 - still under the hint's 3.3 to 4.8.
    YIELD_AFTER = {% if flag?(:darwin) %}1024{% else %}128{% end %}

    # The core, given away once a collection has released the threads it
    # stopped and the runtime lock: a collector looping `GC.collect` on one
    # core stopped every other thread for nearly all its time, and one ran
    # only when a timeslice ended in the instants between two stops. The
    # lock's turn needs the waiter to run at all.
    def self.yield_to_stopped : Nil
      yield_cpu if @@count > 1_i64
    end

    # One turn of the runtime lock's spin past its first look
    # (`IyiRuntimeLock.lock`): answers the turns so far, negative once this
    # spinner is counted on the lock's word, and the generation - the
    # word's count of releases - its last 128 turns began at. A spinner
    # holds nothing, so this is also where a thread asked to stop parks.
    #
    # Every 128th turn from `YIELD_AFTER` on gives the core away: a holder
    # preempted with the lock held otherwise waited out the spinners'
    # timeslices, and 48 threads taking it on twelve cores took 30 times
    # what one thread took for all their turns. That is not fairness. A
    # holder on one core runs its unlock and its next lock back to back,
    # and a spinner runs only while the holder is preempted, so with the
    # lock held: under `start /affinity 1` a thread taking the lock in a
    # loop kept ten threads started beside it waiting 22 s in all, and with
    # 2,000 increments held per turn for more than a minute. So a spinner
    # that saw no release in its 128 turns counts itself, and a free lock
    # with one counted is not taken at a first look: the newcomer gives the
    # core away once, and the counted spinner takes it, uncounting itself
    # in the same CAS. The ten threads: 0.7 to 1 s. Counted on 128 turns of
    # a held word alone, a spinner merely beaten to a moving lock counted
    # too, and the 48 threads took 2.5 to 5 times one; and a take by CAS of
    # the whole word failed on every release between its read and it, past
    # 5 times. An OR of bit 0 takes any free lock: 1.6 to 1.9 times, the
    # plain lock 1.4 to 1.7 on the same machine, and alone a take and
    # release cost the same.
    def self.lock_turn(word : Pointer(Atomic(UInt64)), seen : UInt64, spins : Int64, since : UInt64) : Tuple(Int64, UInt64)
      if spins != 0
        line = @@line
        pay_turn(line, 1_u64) if line != 0 && IyiHeap.read64(line + IYI_TL_LOCKWAIT) == 3_u64
      end
      counted = spins < 0
      turn = (counted ? 0_i64 &- spins : spins) &+ 1
      released = seen.unsafe_shr(16_u64)
      if turn == 1
        since = released
        line = @@line
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_LOCKWAIT, 1_u64) if line != 0
        yield_cpu if (seen & 0xFFFE_u64) != 0_u64
      elsif turn & 127 == 0
        if !counted && released == since
          word.value.add(2_u64)
          counted = true
        end
        since = released
        yield_cpu if turn >= YIELD_AFTER
      end
      # The park after the turn, so the first thing a thread does after
      # a park here is try the lock (`owe_turn`).
      park_if_requested
      {counted ? 0_i64 &- turn : turn, since}
    end


    # This thread's line, from the thread-local or, on Linux in the
    # instant before a child names its own, by its tid. A darwin thread
    # wrote it before any stop could name it.
    def self.own_line : UInt64
      line = @@line
      {% if flag?(:linux) %}
        return line if line != 0
        handle = __iyi_conc_syscall3(IYI_SYS_GETTID, 0_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64).to_u64
        cursor = @@lines
        while cursor != 0
          return cursor if IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_HANDLE) == handle
          cursor = IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_NEXT)
        end
        0_u64
      {% else %}
        line
      {% end %}
    end

    # The handler. Inside the allocator, leave the request and return:
    # `take` parks on its way out. Otherwise copy the registers the kernel
    # saved, record where the thread stopped, and park.
    def self.stopped_by_signal(context : UInt64) : Nil
      line = own_line
      return if line == 0
      if IyiHeap.in_allocator?
        IyiHeap.request_stop
        return
      end
      {% if flag?(:linux) %}
        regs = context + IYI_UC_REGS
        count = IYI_UC_COUNT
        sp = IyiHeap.read64(context + IYI_UC_SP)
        pc = IyiHeap.read64(context + IYI_UC_PC)
      {% else %}
        mcontext = IyiHeap.read64(context + IYI_UC_MCONTEXT)
        regs = mcontext + IYI_MC_REGS
        count = IYI_MC_COUNT
        sp = IyiHeap.read64(mcontext + IYI_MC_SP)
        pc = IyiHeap.read64(mcontext + IYI_MC_PC)
      {% end %}
      index = 0_u64
      while index < count
        IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_SPILL + index * 8_u64, IyiHeap.read64(regs + index * 8_u64))
        index = index + 1_u64
      end
      IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_SP, sp)
      IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_PC, pc)
      park(line)
    end

    # A voluntary stop, from `take`'s exit or the lock's spin: the
    # callee-saved registers are the ones that may hold a value across
    # this call, spilled onto the line the way `IyiRoots` spills the
    # collector's; the caller-saved ones are on the stack by convention.
    #
    # Which registers those are is the platform's ABI, and Windows x64
    # adds rsi and rdi to System V's six. This spilled System V's set on
    # Windows too, so a value held across the deferred stop in rsi or rdi
    # - its only reference - was no root, and the mark could miss it.
    # `IyiRoots.spill_registers` already had the Windows set; this has
    # it now.
    @[NoInline]
    def self.stop_here : Nil
      line = @@line
      return if line == 0
      spill = line + IYI_TL_SPILL
      {% if flag?(:win32) && flag?(:x86_64) %}
        asm("movq %rbx, 0($0)
             movq %rbp, 8($0)
             movq %rdi, 16($0)
             movq %rsi, 24($0)
             movq %r12, 32($0)
             movq %r13, 40($0)
             movq %r14, 48($0)
             movq %r15, 56($0)" :: "{rcx}"(spill) : "memory" : "volatile")
      {% elsif flag?(:x86_64) %}
        asm("movq %rbx, 0($0)
             movq %rbp, 8($0)
             movq %r12, 16($0)
             movq %r13, 24($0)
             movq %r14, 32($0)
             movq %r15, 40($0)" :: "{rdi}"(spill) : "memory" : "volatile")
      {% else %}
        asm("stp x19, x20, [$0, #0]
             stp x21, x22, [$0, #16]
             stp x23, x24, [$0, #32]
             stp x25, x26, [$0, #48]
             stp x27, x28, [$0, #64]
             stp x29, x30, [$0, #80]" :: "{x0}"(spill) : "memory" : "volatile")
      {% end %}
      here = uninitialized UInt64
      IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_SP, pointerof(here).address)
      IyiHeap.write64(line + IYI_TL_PC, 0_u64)
      park(line)
    end

    # Counted in, held until the stopper lets every parked thread go at
    # once, counted out. On Linux and darwin the hold is a futex on the
    # release word: the kernel sleeps the thread only while the word
    # still reads 0, so a release between the look and the sleep is not
    # slept through, and one wake releases every thread parked.
    #
    # darwin parked on a pipe per thread, made when the thread was and
    # never closed, and released by a write to each: two descriptors for
    # every thread a program ever started, so under a terminal's
    # `ulimit -n` of 256 bench/collect_trigger.sh's five hundred
    # short-lived threads died at "pipe failed", and thread_exercise's at
    # "kqueue failed" when the pipes had left a thread's poller one
    # descriptor short. libSystem's futex is public from macOS 14.4, which
    # the mark helpers' park already needs. Here it runs in the stop's
    # signal handler, where `read` had POSIX's promise and this has none
    # in writing; what it does is read off its disassembly (macOS 26.6):
    # it checks its arguments and makes the one `__ulock_wait2` system
    # call, holding no lock and touching nothing but the word and, when
    # it fails, errno - which is what made `read` safe here too. A handler
    # that changes errno changes it under the code it interrupted, between
    # a failed call and its look at errno, so errno is put back. And a
    # wait answers EFAULT at once when the word's page is not resident
    # (an untouched word did, in C on the M2); the loop's read brings the
    # page back and waits again.
    def self.park(line : UInt64) : Nil
      page = control
      owe_turn(line, page)
      count_in(page + IYI_TC_SUSPENDED)
      {% if flag?(:linux) %}
        while word(page + IYI_TC_RESUME).value.get == 0_u64
          __iyi_conc_syscall6(IYI_SYS_FUTEX, (page + IYI_TC_RESUME).to_i64, IYI_FUTEX_WAIT, 0_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64, 0_i64)
        end
      {% elsif flag?(:darwin) %}
        errno = LibC.error_location.value
        while word(page + IYI_TC_RESUME).value.get == 0_u64
          LibC.os_sync_wait_on_address(Pointer(Void).new(page + IYI_TC_RESUME), 0_u64, 8_u64, 0_u32)
        end
        LibC.error_location.value = errno
      {% elsif flag?(:win32) %}
        while word(page + IYI_TC_RESUME).value.get == 0_u64
          yield_cpu
        end
      {% end %}
      count_in(page + IYI_TC_RESUMED)
    end

    # ── Roots ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    # Every stopped thread's registers and stack, for the collector. The
    # stack's top is the mapping's the sp is in: a fiber's, asked of every
    # fiber, or the thread's own.
    def self.each_stopped_root(visit : Proc(UInt64, Nil)) : Nil
      own = @@line
      cursor = @@lines
      while cursor != 0
        if cursor != own
          IyiRoots.scan_range(cursor + IYI_TL_SPILL, cursor + IYI_TL_SPILL_END, visit)
          sp = IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_SP)
          top = IyiScheduler.fiber_top_holding(sp)
          top = IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_TOP) if top == 0
          {% if flag?(:win32) %}
            sp = first_written(sp, top)
          {% end %}
          IyiRoots.scan_range(sp, top, visit)
        end
        cursor = IyiHeap.read64(cursor + IYI_TL_NEXT)
      end
    end

    {% if flag?(:win32) %}
      # Where a stopped thread's stack starts holding anything: *sp*, or the
      # first committed page above it that is not a guard page. A thread, or
      # a task on a fiber stack, stopped after its `sub rsp` or push moved
      # sp below StackLimit and before its first touch of the new page has
      # sp in that stack's PAGE_GUARD page. Only the owner's touch grows a
      # stack; the collector's raised STATUS_GUARD_PAGE_VIOLATION in the
      # collector and cleared the guard. On a thread's stack nothing claims
      # that, and the process died with 0x80000001 and nothing printed: ten
      # runs in ten of one thread collecting while new threads recursed
      # 2,000 frames, inside 0.65 s. On a fiber's the overflow handler named
      # it, and a task 1,500 frames into its 256 KiB was reported a "stack
      # overflow" (6 runs in 20). Nothing below a guard page was ever
      # written, so the scan begins above it.
      def self.first_written(sp : UInt64, top : UInt64) : UInt64
        buffer = uninitialized UInt64[6]
        info = pointerof(buffer).as(Pointer(UInt64))
        while sp < top && LibC.VirtualQuery(Pointer(Void).new(sp), info.as(Void*), 48_u64) != 0_u64
          # MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION: the base, the size at 24, the state
          # at 32 (MEM_COMMIT 0x1000) and the protection at 36 (PAGE_GUARD
          # 0x100).
          return sp if (info[4] & 0xFFFFFFFF_u64) == 0x1000_u64 && (info[4].unsafe_shr(32_u64) & 0x100_u64) == 0_u64
          sp = info[0] + info[3]
        end
        sp
      end
    {% end %}
  end
{% end %}
